<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Parisii (Britain)</h1><p class="st-meta">100 BCE – 71 CE (approx.) · capital Petuaria (Brough-on-Humber)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">East Yorkshire people known for the Arras culture of square-barrow and chariot burials, sharing their name with the Parisii of Gaul.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/parisii">Parisii</a> are identified with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras-culture">Arras culture</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire-wolds">Yorkshire Wolds</a>, which from about the 4th century BCE buried its dead under small square barrows, sometimes with dismantled two-wheeled vehicles, as at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wetwang">Wetwang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garton-slack">Garton Slack</a>. The likeness of these burials to rites in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, and the shared name with the Parisii of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>, has fuelled long debate over migration or contact.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Society was based on mixed farming on the chalk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire-wolds">Wolds</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vale-of-pickering">Vale of Pickering</a>, with large cemeteries of thousands of barrows. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">Chariot</a> burials of men and women, with harness fittings, mirrors and brooches, point to an elite who displayed status through horses and fine metalwork. No coinage is attributed to them and no rulers are named.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The area came under Roman control during the campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-petillius-cerialis">Petillius Cerialis</a> against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/brigantes">Brigantes</a> from 71. Ptolemy later listed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petuaria">Petuaria</a> as their town, and an inscription from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brough-east-riding-of-yorkshire">Brough-on-Humber</a> names an aedile of the vicus of Petuaria; the Parisi probably formed a small civitas of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/parisii-britain.jpg" alt="Parisii (Britain)" width="320" height="453"><figcaption>Image: Map_of_the_Territory_of_the_Dobunni.svg: *Map_of_the_Territory_of_the_Cornovii_(Midlands).svg: *Map_of_the_Territory_... · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Territory_of_the_Parisii.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 BCE – 71 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Petuaria (Brough-on-Humber)</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Parisii</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-14&amp;focus=parisii-britain" aria-label="Parisii (Britain) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4667 332 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Parisii (Britain) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4987 588L5304 942M4987 588L5020 640"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5020 640h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4987 588h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4987 588h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Petuaria. <a href="/atlas?year=-14&amp;focus=parisii-britain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
